{
  "identifier": "NG257",
  "full_title": "The Presentation in the Temple",
  "short_title": "The Presentation in the Temple",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "Gothic",
  "material": "oil, originally on wood, transferred to canvas",
  "dimensions": "98.4 × 70.2 cm",
  "production_date": "likely early 1480s-1490",
  "brief_description": "This is one of the few surviving intact scenes which once formed part of an altarpiece made for the high altar of the Benedictine abbey at Liesborn in the west of Germany. This painting is likely to have originally appeared to the right of the main scene showing the Crucifixion.\n\nShortly after birth, Jewish boys were required by religious law to be presented at the temple to be consecrated.  Here, the Virgin Mary is about to hand Christ over to the priest. This is Simeon, who is described in the Gospel of Luke as recognising Christ's divinity and significance at this moment (Luke 2: 28--30). Simeon was one of the saints to whom the altarpiece was dedicated.\n\nThe artist has set this biblical event in a contemporary setting that would be familiar to worshippers. The temple, for example, is represented as a Gothic church with a high vaulted roof and decorative leaded windows.",
  "accession_date": "1854-01-01",
